<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Revolution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Revolutions of 1989</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1989</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Autumn of Nations, Fall of communism</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The revolutions of 1989 were the wave of largely peaceful uprisings that overthrew the communist regimes of Central and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-europe">Eastern Europe</a>. Beginning with negotiated change in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, where the trade union Solidarity won partly free elections in June, they spread to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-germany">East Germany</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia</a>, and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a> ended in bloodshed and the execution of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicolae-ceausescu">Nicolae Ceaușescu</a>. They brought down the Iron Curtain, led to the reunification of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> in 1990 and opened the way to the dissolution of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> in 1991.</p></div>
